course: dictionary
lesson: 12354
title: Creditable: good enough to praise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Creditable means good enough to deserve praise and respect, even when it is not the very best. «She came third in the race, a creditable result after being ill all week.» Third is not first, but the result was good and honourable, especially because she was ill.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Words like effort, result, or place tell you that someone did something, and creditable tells you how well they did it. Try your meaning in the sentence: "a good result that deserves praise" still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: Creditable is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in reports and news. Teachers write it in school reports, and sports writers use it too. «Our team put up a creditable fight against the champions.» They lost, but they played well, and that deserves respect.
fact: Creditable means deserving praise, good enough to be respected, though often not the very best.
fact: «A creditable third place» means third place is a good result that deserves praise.
fact: «A creditable effort» means someone tried hard and did well, even if they did not win.
fact: Creditable is a formal word, so it is common in school reports and sports news.
fact: A rare meaning, about money, is able to be added to an account as credit.
exercise: «Ravi came a creditable third in the swimming race.» What does creditable mean here? | Last, and very slow | Surprising and strange | Wet and cold | *Good, and deserving praise
exercise: The teacher wrote, «A creditable attempt at a hard puzzle.» What does the teacher think about the attempt? | It was silly and careless | It was finished in one minute | *It was good and deserves respect, even though it was not perfect | It was copied from a book
exercise: Which sentence uses creditable correctly? | Creditable, I ate my dinner in the garden. | *The team gave a creditable performance, losing only by one goal. | The creditable cat slept all afternoon on the mat. | The bus was creditable and left on time.
test: «She finished the marathon in a creditable time for her first race.» What does creditable suggest about her time? | It was the slowest time ever | It was made up by a computer | It was too short to count | *It was good, and she should be praised for it
test: Dad says, «That is a creditable effort, son.» What is Dad telling his son? | His effort was wasted | He should try again tomorrow | *His effort was good and worth praise | He has won the game
exam: Read this: «Mia's drawing was not the best in the class, but it was creditable.» What does creditable tell you about Mia's drawing? | It was the best drawing in the class | *It was good and deserved praise, though not the very best | It was too small to see | It was copied from a magazine
